# Data Retention and Deletion Standard — R-5 Erasure requests

> Module ErasureRequest

This module answers: by when must personal data be deleted on a data
subject's request for erasure?

It is the densest judgement cluster in the corpus — four judgement inputs
govern one 30-day arithmetic — and the reason both its clauses are HYBRID.
Encoded as plain rules, the module would assert a deletion due date in
precisely the cases where the law forbids deletion, and would assert one for
a request nobody has yet held to be valid.

`deletion_due_date` is an `optional of date`, and `Absent` means **no
deletion deadline has arisen**: because the request has not been held valid,
because it has not been verified, or because R-5.2 disapplies the deadline
altogether. `deletion_refused` distinguishes the last of those, which is the
only one of the three that is a refusal.

## Prologue — declarations

`erasure_request_valid` is the **judgement input** of R-5.1: whether a
request is a "valid request for erasure" turns on the requester's standing,
the ground relied on, and whether the request is manifestly unfounded or
excessive, none of which this Standard defines.

`retention_required_by_legal_obligation` and
`retention_required_for_legal_claims` are the two **judgement inputs** of
R-5.2's first and second limbs. Its third limb, a Legal Hold, is computable,
and arrives as `legal_hold_blocks_erasure` — which is
`LegalHold.HoldEffect.erasure_blocked_by_hold`, so it already carries R-4.2's
own judgement that applicable data protection law permits the retention. A
plain "is a hold in force" input here would let a hold defeat an erasure
request unconditionally, which is what R-4.2 says it does not do.

`verification_date` is `Absent` until the Company has verified the request.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope ErasureDeadline:
  input request_date content date
  input verification_date content optional of date
  input erasure_request_valid content boolean
  input retention_required_by_legal_obligation content boolean
  input retention_required_for_legal_claims content boolean
  input legal_hold_blocks_erasure content boolean
  output deletion_due_date content optional of date
  output deletion_refused content boolean
```

| NO-CLAUSE: the rounding mode is a compiler directive, not a rule. Only the
| 30 days of R-5.1 are added in this scope, which as a day duration is never
| ambiguous, but the mode is declared so the arithmetic is total by
| construction; `date round down` matches the other DATA-RET modules.
|
| The assertion is a well-formedness invariant on the inputs, not a rule of
| the Standard: a request cannot be verified before it is made. It is what
| `request_date` is for. R-5.1 measures its 30 days from verification and
| not from the request, so the request date enters no computed date at all,
| and holding both dates is how a reader can see that.

```catala
scope ErasureDeadline:
  date round down
  assertion
    (match verification_date with pattern
     -- Absent: true
     -- Present content verified: verified >= request_date)
```

## R-5 Erasure requests

| DATA-RET R-5.1 (006-data-retention-standard.md:81)
|
| Where a data subject makes a valid request for erasure, the Company will
| delete the relevant personal data within 30 days of verifying the request.

**HYBRID.** The deadline is exact; validity is not. So the base case is that
no deadline has arisen, and the deadline is an exception that fires only on
the judgement that the request is valid. This is the whole point of the
HYBRID treatment: the module must not assert a 30-day deletion deadline for a
request nobody has validated.

The 30 days run from **verification**, not from the request. A valid request
that has not yet been verified therefore has no deadline either — the clause
gives the clock no start — and `Absent` is returned for that reason as well.

A request that is valid and not caught by R-5.2 is one the Company will
delete, so it is not refused. R-5.2 supplies the only ground of refusal.

```catala
scope ErasureDeadline:
  label r5_1_none definition deletion_due_date equals Absent

  label r5_1 exception r5_1_none definition deletion_due_date
    under condition erasure_request_valid
    consequence equals
      match verification_date with pattern
      -- Absent: Absent
      -- Present content verified: Present content (verified + 30 day)

  label r5_1_not_refused definition deletion_refused equals false
```

| DATA-RET R-5.2 (006-data-retention-standard.md:84)
|
| By way of exception to R-5.1, the Company will not delete personal data
| where it is required to retain it to comply with a legal obligation, for
| the establishment or exercise of legal claims, or where a Legal Hold
| applies.

**HYBRID**, and an exception to R-5.1 at the depth the text states, because
it disapplies the R-5.1 deadline entirely. Its three limbs are alternatives,
so any one of them suffices.

Both definitions repeat the parent's condition `erasure_request_valid`. A
Catala exception does not inherit its parent's condition, and without the
repetition R-5.2 would fire on an invalid request too — reporting the
Company as *refusing* a request it never held to be valid, which the clause
does not say and which misdescribes the record. R-5.2 is an exception to
R-5.1; it has no application where R-5.1 does not.

```catala
scope ErasureDeadline:
  label r5_2 exception r5_1 definition deletion_due_date
    under condition
      erasure_request_valid
      or (retention_required_by_legal_obligation
           or retention_required_for_legal_claims
           or legal_hold_blocks_erasure)
    consequence equals Absent

  label r5_2_refused exception r5_1_not_refused definition deletion_refused
    under condition
      erasure_request_valid
      and (retention_required_by_legal_obligation
           or retention_required_for_legal_claims
           or legal_hold_blocks_erasure)
    consequence equals true
```
